\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Exact support/exponent ledger. The point-cardinality reduction at \(q=2\) is \((3,1)\), the sole locked row/type pair combining source support and unit exponent. No reduction type does so uniformly across all nine rows; the retained support still fails to identify the modulus because \(r_2=r_4=3\). Entries are exact and use the inverse Artin--Mazur sign convention.}}{10}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:nine-row-ledger}{{2}{10}{Exact support/exponent ledger. The point-cardinality reduction at \(q=2\) is \((3,1)\), the sole locked row/type pair combining source support and unit exponent. No reduction type does so uniformly across all nine rows; the retained support still fails to identify the modulus because \(r_2=r_4=3\). Entries are exact and use the inverse Artin--Mazur sign convention}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {7.1}What the finite rows show}{11}{subsection.7.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{eq:period-collisions}{{40}{11}{What the finite rows show}{equation.7.40}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Exact nine-row retention ledger in the frozen order $q=(2,3,5,7,11,4,6,9,10)$. Panel A shows the integer triples $(n_q,r_q,m_q)$ with $m_q=n_q/r_q$; panel B marks exactly the collisions $r_2=r_4=3$ and $r_6=r_9=12$; panel C lists all four scalar support--exponent pairs. The cell $(3,1)^\star $ at $q=2$ is the sole locked row/type pair with source support and unit exponent. There is no family-uniform starred column, and $r_2=r_4$ shows why the $q=2$ exception is not modulus-specific. These finite rows are exact implementation and falsification controls, not a fit or an all-$q$ inference.}}{12}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:nine-row-retention}{{2}{12}{Exact nine-row retention ledger in the frozen order $q=(2,3,5,7,11,4,6,9,10)$. Panel A shows the integer triples $(n_q,r_q,m_q)$ with $m_q=n_q/r_q$; panel B marks exactly the collisions $r_2=r_4=3$ and $r_6=r_9=12$; panel C lists all four scalar support--exponent pairs. The cell $(3,1)^\star $ at $q=2$ is the sole locked row/type pair with source support and unit exponent. There is no family-uniform starred column, and $r_2=r_4$ shows why the $q=2$ exception is not modulus-specific. These finite rows are exact implementation and falsification controls, not a fit or an all-$q$ inference}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Effectivity controls labelled-twist recovery but does not turn quotient or inertia data into return times. Panel A is a regular orbit, for which the action kernel is trivial and the labelled twist is recovered exactly. Panel B is the trivial one-point action, whose quotient stack is $BC_q$ with $q$ static inertia sectors and whose twist is visible only modulo the full kernel. Panel C is the separately typed effective control $C_6/C_2\sqcup C_6/C_3$: its labelled twist is recovered, while its source factors occur only at periods $3$ and $2$, never at period $6$. Panel D states the general action-kernel and quotient-stack formulas. This structural control is not an arithmetic modulus row or a new candidate.}}{13}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:effectivity-counterexamples}{{3}{13}{Effectivity controls labelled-twist recovery but does not turn quotient or inertia data into return times. Panel A is a regular orbit, for which the action kernel is trivial and the labelled twist is recovered exactly. Panel B is the trivial one-point action, whose quotient stack is $BC_q$ with $q$ static inertia sectors and whose twist is visible only modulo the full kernel. Panel C is the separately typed effective control $C_6/C_2\sqcup C_6/C_3$: its labelled twist is recovered, while its source factors occur only at periods $3$ and $2$, never at period $6$. Panel D states the general action-kernel and quotient-stack formulas. This structural control is not an arithmetic modulus row or a new candidate}{figure.3}{}}
